  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 235 PM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A surface low has tracked north through southern New \nEngland and into southern Vermont today, bringing an area of rain \nback into the region. The rain will attempt to continue north, but \nthe low will be occluding and weakening. Rainfall rates with the \nband continue to decrease, with peak hourly amounts mostly around \nand below 0.3 inches. The rain will continue to lighten as the \nafternoon and evening progress, though some light rain will likely \nre-enter northern areas. Overall, around 1-3 inches has fallen, with \nadditional amounts up to around an inch possible. Outside the main \narea of low pressure, areas of convection developed in the immediate \nSt. Lawrence Valley this morning. A narrow strip of 2-3 inches has \nfallen in the Ogdensburg area and south, but it has not been enough \nto cause any flooding. The remaining convection is currently in the \nprocess of weakening. The terrain there is not very susceptible to \nflash flooding and it would take notably more rain to cause any. As \nthe main area of low pressure makes its way north, the synoptic rain \nand clouds will minimize the instability, and subsidence in the \nareas just outside the shield of precipitation should start to \ndampen any additional convection as well. While widespread rainfall \nwill continue into tomorrow, it will become scattered and mostly \nconfined to eastern areas, except in the afternoon when diurnal \nheating will cause some weak convection. Overall, the flood threat \nhas decreased. While an isolated area of flash flooding still cannot \nbe completely ruled out in southern/central Vermont and Essex \nCounty, it is looking increasingly less likely.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: The upper-level low becomes cut off and gradually exits \nheading through Friday, though the dynamics will be too weak and too \nfar east to produce any more significant precipitation. However, \nisolated showers are possible on Friday due to the diurnal heating. \nRidging briefly becomes established on Saturday, and the day should \nbe mostly dry. Temperatures will be seasonable during this stretch, \nwith highs in the 70s to mid 80s and lows in the 50s and low 60s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: On Sunday and Monday, showers and thunderstorms will move \nalong a trough of low pressure. While the air mass will have plenty of \nmoisture to tap into, we are looking at quick moving air mass \nthunderstorms. Given the wet antecedent conditions, we will \ncontinue to monitor this system for any potential impacts.\n\nAs the trough exits our region, high pressure will build in with dry \nand stable air. Tuesday and Wednesday will be significantly warmer than \naverage, with valley locations reaching int the mid 80s for daytime \nhighs and mid 50s to mid 60s for nighttime lows.",
